f people  like corey gunselman  who manages one liberty place it's been  a problem honestly  we do notice the collisions that do  happen thanks  to a trade group building managers like  gunselman finally heard keith russell's  pitch  they could save birds and some energy by  turning off the exterior lighting that  seems to confuse birds at night  gunselman signed on and we have new  controls that we input  lights off at midnight only april  through may  this is what it has looked like this  spring as the most prominent buildings  in philadelphia's skyline  darken their lights and let the birds  follow the stars lights out philadelphia  follows similar programs  in nearly 40 other american cities they  use the stars in part to navigate  which might explain why they're  attracted to lights in some cases  jason wextein an ornithologist at the  academy of natural sciences in  philadelphia  says there's reason to think the dark  sky will help we know in other places  there are significant differences this  building mccormick place on the  chicago lakefront many fewer birds are  dying  but skyscrapers make up a tiny  percentage of all buildings  ordinary homes end up killing many more  birds  you find a few different ways to  approach yeah absolutely there's acid  etching here  so damien ruffner at philadelphia's  discovery center  has suggestions it comes in this  roll of tape small vinyl dots can break  up the reflection  so can p chord any vertical lines four  inches or less  but intentionally blocking a view that  can be a tough sell  we've seen these other solutions that go  right on the glass  etching dots these types of things are  any of those possibilities for you guys  that is something that we definitely  could look into the one thing is that we  don't want to take away the luster of  our beautiful double pane windows  that's the rub yeah right right but the  visibility keith russell cares most  about  is that his city finally seems to notice  its birds  this is something you've cared about for  a long time it must be gratifying to see  the general public start to care about  this too  it's really amazing after having worked  you know in this area for so long really  warms my heart  it makes me very proud to be a  philadelphian  there were no major bird strikes in  philadelphia this spring migration  the lights will be on again starting  tuesday but when the birds head back  south  in the fall keith russell already has a  promise that the brightest light over  center city  will again be the moon for cbs this  morning saturday brook silva bragg  who knew makes sense right yeah yeah and  then there's ways to  fix it yeah try to fix it for the future  i'm so glad they're thinking about it  right yeah

